{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>For on this day shall atonement be made for you, to cleanse you.</b> This verse is a promise for all generations, that Yom HaKippurim is a day singled out for pardon and atonement, and on it Israel are cleansed of all their sins that they committed before HaShem. And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: transgressions between a person and the Place — Yom Kippur atones; but those between a person and his fellow — Yom Kippur does not atone until he appeases his fellow. And when the High Priest would make confession on Yom Kippur he would say this verse in his prayer, and the Name that he would pronounce in this verse is the Name of forty-two, and therefore we say in our prayer on Yom Kippur ״לפני ה׳ תטהרו״ — that is, before the Name of forty-two. And so is the opinion of my master R. Shem Tov, and so is the primary view, for Yom Kippur is a day of mercy within judgment, and this Name has seven words, each and every word of six letters; the six words are the attribute of mercy and the seventh is the attribute of judgment, and the High Priest needed to supplicate before the Judge." "<b>And know</b> that the Name of forty-two is that by which the world was created, and it emerges from בראשית with its known vowels, and it is traced from the attribute of Gevurah. And the first three letters are the very first three letters of אלפ״א בית״א, to teach you that in six days the world was created, in six directions, with six seals — as I explained in the section of Genesis at the verse of the sixth day. And the seventh word begins with שי״ן, which means fire, and this is the שי״ן that trills, which is the attribute of judgment, and the rule of this word is that it is pointed with shureq and in it the entire Name is sealed. For thus you will find them alluded to in the conclusion of this section: it immediately juxtaposes שבת שבתון היא — the קו״ף is fixed holiness, and it is the spirit of holiness, and this is what it says: ולבש את בגדי הבד בגדי הקדש — the הוא״ו in the verse after it: וכפר את מקדש הקדש — and the conclusion of the section: כאשר צוה ה׳ את משה — and understand all of this." "<b>And there are</b> among the great ones whose opinion is that the Explicit Name (השם המיוחד) is what he would pronounce, and the Gaon our Master Saadiah of blessed memory is among them. And they say that what we recite — לפני ה׳ תטהרו — is because on the day when we say ״and thus he would say,״ and we testify about him and about what he would say, how could we testify about him that he would say לפני ה׳ תטהרו — that is, before the Name that we are not permitted to pronounce as written? But he was permitted in this, for he would pronounce it ten times in the Sanctuary on Yom Kippur. And so they said in tractate Yoma, chapter טרף בקלפי: our Rabbis taught — ten times the priest pronounces the Name on that day: three in the first confession, and three in the second confession, and three over the scapegoat, and one over the lots. And so you will find in this section ten instances of the Explicit Name from ויאמר ה׳ אל משה until the completion of the section. But the first opinion is the primary one for us: that the Name of forty-two is what he would pronounce, as I mentioned above. And from this we say in the text of that prayer: וכשהיו שומעין את שם המפורש יוצא מפי כהן גדול בקדושה ובטהרה היו כורעים ומשתחוים ונופלים על פניהם ואומרים ברוך שם כבוד מלכותו לעולם ועד. And the Name was not pronounced in holiness except from within thought." "<b>And thus</b> said the masters of the Chariot: the Names of the Holy One, blessed be He, are not pronounced in holiness except from within what the heart of man hands down. And their intention is to say that if they go out on his lips, they spread into the air, and the air becomes subject to impurity, and the Name of Heaven is profaned. Therefore the priest would swallow it — he would begin to pronounce it with the emission of the lips and then complete it by swallowing it." "<b>And I have heard</b> that the swallowing was in certain words in which the letters of the הוי״ה appear together with their vowels, such as אליהו הנביא, ישעיהו הנביא, and similar — and this was the swallowing, and this is the meaning of מבליעין that our Rabbis of blessed memory said in chapter עשרה יוחסין: the Name of twelve — they do not hand it over except to the discreet, and the discreet among the priesthood swallow it in the melodies of their brothers the priests. And the Name of forty-two he would pronounce with the emission of his lips, in its letters and in its received vowels. And according to the opinion that says the Explicit Name is what he would pronounce, he too would pronounce it with the emission of his lips in its letters and in its received vowels, even though the essence of the holiness in pronouncing the Name הוי״ה is only in its vowels. And it is a tradition in the hands of the masters of truth that they do not hand over the Name except over water, as it is said (Psalms 29): ״קול ה׳ על המים״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:19.948967019Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}